          %h3#remark Remark
          %p
            With free objects, one is often primarily interested in taking quotients whence a free category over a graph is usually a somewhat auxiliary gadget its main interest lying in the role it plays in defining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fractions"} categories of fractions
            (cf.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Zisman"} Gabriel-Zisman
            1967 pp.1,6; probably the original source of the path category in this sense).
          %p
            But similar techniques apply to various notions of graphs or more restricted classes of categories with forgetful functors to graphs permitting to syntactically generate various notions of free categories with extra structure and in some of these cases it occurs that the corresponding free category (of paths) is indeed interesting in itself e.g. the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+topos"} free topos
            arises this way and the syntactic derivations of context free grammars arrange themselves into such categories of paths as explored in Walters (
            %a{:href => "#Walters1"}> 1989a
            ,
            %a{:href => "#Walters2"}> 1989b
            ) or Latch (
            %a{:href => "#Latch"}> 1991
            ).
          %h2#path_category_of_a_space Path category of a space
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (or some other kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            with a notion of maps from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intervals"} intervals
            into it), there are various ways to obtain a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            whose objects are the points of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and whose morphisms are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paths"} paths
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %em path category
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In particular, for every topological space there is its
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
                whose morphisms are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
                classes of paths in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+space"} directed space
                there is a notion of path category called the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+category"} fundamental category
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                When
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth space
                %em thin
                homotopy. This yields a notion of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+groupoid"} path groupoid
            %li
              %p
                If
                %em parameterized
                paths are used, there is a way to get a category of paths without dividing out any equivalence relation: this is the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+path+category"} Moore path category
          %h2#arrow_category Arrow category
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [I,X]
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval category
            might be called a “directed path category of
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space"} path space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+category"} arrow category
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and sometimes even denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Arr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Arr(X)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concatenation"} concatenation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+groupoid"} free groupoid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+diagram"} free diagram
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+monoid"}> free monoid
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+operad"} free operad
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+topos"} free topos
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+category"} free category
            on a graph has a section in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
              ,
              %em Handbook of categorical Algebra vol. 1
              , Cambridge UP 1994.
          %p or in
          %ul
            %li#MacLane
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
              ,
              %em Categories for the Working Mathematician
              , Springer 1998.
          %p The following is another source for this, even an open source:
          %ul
            %li#BarrWells
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Barr"} Michael Barr
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Wells"} Charles Wells
              %em Category Theory for Computing Science
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/triples/Barr-Wells-ctcs.pdf"} PDF
          %p The path categories of context free grammars are explored in
          %ul
            %li#Latch
              %p
                Dana Latch,
                %em The connection between the fundamental groupoid and a unification algorithm for syntactil algebras (extended abstract)
                , Cah. Top. Géom. Diff. Cat.
                %strong XXXII
                (1991). (
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CTGDC_1991__32_3_203_0"}> link
                )
            %li#Walters1
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Walters"}> Bob Walters
                ,
                %em The free category with products on a multigraph
                , JPAA
                %strong 62
                (1989). (
                %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022404989901527"}> link
                )
            %li#Walters2
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bob+Walters"}> Bob Walters
                ,
                %em A note on context-free languages
                , JPAA
                %strong 62
                (1989). (
                %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022404989901515"}> link
                )
          %p
            Since the perspective on categories as ‘graphs with operations’ is closely related to their ‘logico-deductive’ side, it figures prominently in the work of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"} Jim Lambek
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phil+Scott"} Phil Scott
            (cf. the references at
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+topos"} free topos
